Here is the complete, absolute reference of all official ffuf command-line flags (rules) categorized by function. Use this as your exhaustive blueprint for fine-tuning bug bounty scans. [1, 2] 
## 🌐 HTTP Request Options
These flags dictate how ffuf builds, formats, and transmits the network payload. [2, 3] 

* 
* -u : Target URL (e.g., https://target.com/FUZZ). [Mandatory].
* -X : HTTP method to use (e.g., GET, POST, PUT) (Default: GET).
* -H : Custom HTTP header in "Name: Value" format. Can use multiple times.
* -b : Cookie data string ("NAME1=V1; NAME2=V2") for authenticated sessions.
* -d : POST request data payload.
* -http2 : Enforces the use of the HTTP2 protocol (Default: false).
* -ignore-body : Drops response body parsing. Drastically speeds up asset mapping by focusing only on headers/status codes.
* -r : Follow HTTP location redirects automatically (Default: false).
* -raw : Prevents URL-encoding on the target URI path. Sends the payload byte-for-byte.
* -sni : Targets a static TLS Server Name Indication (Does not support FUZZ syntax). [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8] 
* 

## 🎯 Matchers (-m...)
Matchers specify exactly what criteria a response must meet to be outputted to the screen. [4, 9] 

* 
* -mc : Match specific HTTP status codes (e.g., -mc 200,301,302).
* -ml : Match a specific number of lines in the response.
* -mr : Match a specific Regular Expression string within the body text.
* -ms : Match an exact response payload byte size.
* -mw : Match a precise word count inside the response.
* -mt : Match responses based on milliseconds elapsed (response time target). [4, 5, 8, 9, 10] 
* 

## 🚫 Filters (-f...)
Filters specify exactly what criteria a response must be dropped or ignored to remove noise. [4, 11] 

* 
* -fc : Filter out specific HTTP status codes (e.g., -fc 404,403).
* -fl : Filter out responses with a specific number of lines.
* -fr : Filter out responses matching a specific Regular Expression.
* -fs : Filter out responses matching an exact byte size.
* -fw : Filter out responses matching a precise word count.
* -ft : Filter out responses matching specific completion times. [4, 5, 9, 10, 12] 
* 

## 📁 Wordlists & Multiple Target Handling
Rules that determine how files and keywords are mapped into memory. [4, 6] 

* 
* -w : Wordlist location format. Format can be /path:KEYWORD (Default keyword is FUZZ). [Mandatory]. [5, 6, 13] 
* -mode : Behavior matrix when managing multiple wordlists simultaneously:
* clusterbomb : Tests every available item configuration combination (Cartesian product).
   * pitchfork : Advances lines concurrently across lists side-by-side (Lockstep).
   * sniper : Swaps wordlists sequence target locations sequentially across fields. [4, 6, 9] 
* -e : Append list of specific file extensions separated by commas (e.g., -e .php,.json,.bak). [5, 12] 
* -ic : Ignores comments or documentation blocks started with # inside wordlists. [13, 14] 
* -enc : Applies payload encoding encoders natively before executing requests.
* 

## 🔄 Recursion Rules

* 
* -recursion : Enables deep directory mapping. When a directory maps successfully, it spins off a sub-fuzzer inside that folder.
* -recursion-depth : Maximum folder nesting limit allowed during discovery sweeps (Default: 0 / unlimited).
* -recursion-strategy : Dictates entry conditions. Options are default (redirect analysis) or greedy (recurse everything matched). [2, 7] 
* 

## 🏎‍🟀 Performance, Throttling & Network Tuning
Critical for compliance with bug bounty rate-limiting regulations. [4, 6] 

* 
* -t : Number of parallel runtime execution threads (Default: 40).
* -p : Absolute artificial delay timer pause injected between requests (e.g., -p 0.1 is 100ms).
* -rate : Strict maximum request limit threshold cap per second (e.g., -rate 20).
* -timeout : Drops connections if the endpoint hangs for a specific period in seconds (Default: 10).
* -maxtime : Caps maximum runtime duration allowance across the entire execution run.
* -maxtime-job : Max execution runtime cap permitted for an isolated target/directory job slice. [1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 15] 
* 

## 🕵️ Proxies & Traffic Inspection

* 
* -x : Upstream Proxy routing definition URL (Supports HTTP or SOCKS5, e.g., http://127.0.0.1:8080).
* -replay-proxy : Forwards only successful matching entries straight to another proxy tool like [Burp Suite](https://portswigger.net/burp) to avoid bloating proxy state context logs. [1, 2, 4] 
* 

## 📝 Logging & Outputs

* 
* -o : Destination path location string to commit results.
* -of : Explicit file format conversion mapping. Supported formats: json, ejson, html, md, csv, all.
* -or : Suppress data backup file creations; only updates file parameters if changes or valid bugs break. [4, 7, 8, 10] 
* 

## ⚙️ Automation & UI Control

* 
* -ac : Dynamic auto-calibration filtering rule. Pre-probes the application to dynamically remove custom site layouts or variations from matching patterns.
* -acc : Extends string configurations defining exact parameters used by -ac calibration loops.
* -config : Ingests external settings structures built directly within custom .toml configuration properties blueprints.
* -s : Silent console mode. Strips status lines, bars, and banners for neat pipeline standard output forwarding.
* -v : Verbose runtime logging output mode. Explicitly prints the translated dynamic query payload targets. [4, 6, 10, 15, 16] 
